description:
Chrome suspends inactive service workers after ~5 minutes. Hamburg's extension lacks a keep-alive mechanism, which can cause WebSocket connections to die during idle periods.

## Problem
- If no tab interaction occurs for ~5 minutes, Chrome suspends the service worker
- This kills the WebSocket connection to Core
- Next MCP call fails silently until user interacts with browser
- Hamburg has reconnect logic, but it only triggers when an event handler runs (which won't happen while suspended)

## Fix
Add periodic keep-alive ping in `DriveSocket` (packages/extension/src/background.ts):

```typescript
private keepAliveTimer: number | null = null;

// In connect() after socket opens:
this.keepAliveTimer = self.setInterval(() => {
  if (this.socket?.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) {
    this.sendEvent("drive.keepalive", {});
  }
}, 30000);

// In stop():
if (this.keepAliveTimer !== null) {
  clearInterval(this.keepAliveTimer);
  this.keepAliveTimer = null;
}
```

## Core Side
Core should acknowledge keepalive events (no-op handler, or just ignore them gracefully).

## Reference
- browser-mcp uses 5-second pings; 30 seconds is likely sufficient
- Comparison report: .context/browser-mcp-comparison.md

